1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:08,207
Resonance MRI, by Nebulytic. Follow the magnetic
field, hydrogen moments and received signal through

2
00:00:08,207 --> 00:00:15,269
a complete simulated scan. The motion is slowed for
explanation. Numerical settings belong to this

3
00:00:15,269 --> 00:00:16,350
teaching model.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:25,875
This teaching model uses a simulated hand to show
how MRI turns magnetic resonance into an image.

5
00:00:26,075 --> 00:00:34,375
These silver windings represent the main magnet.
This model uses a steady current of 133 amperes.

6
00:00:34,575 --> 00:00:39,925
Their field, B₀, runs along the bore at one tesla;
these lines trace it.

7
00:00:40,125 --> 00:00:45,925
These arrows give the directions: z, in violet, runs
along the bore and the field.

8
00:00:46,125 --> 00:00:50,800
y, in orange, points up; x, in cyan, points across
the bore.

9
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:57,200
Inside the magnet sit three gradient coils; each
makes the field change along one direction.

10
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:02,825
Each has its direction's colour: the x coils cyan, y
orange, z violet.

11
00:01:03,025 --> 00:01:08,750
The coral RF coil transmits; the smaller gold loop
receives the tissue’s signal.

12
00:01:08,950 --> 00:01:14,900
A cable carries the receive coil’s voltage into the
sampling and filtering electronics.

13
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:19,875
A hand lies in the bore; the scanner will image it,
slice by slice.

14
00:01:20,917 --> 00:01:26,092
Start with one hydrogen nucleus: its spin gives it a
magnetic moment.

15
00:01:26,292 --> 00:01:33,006
This arrow shows the spin state’s expectation value;
its transverse component precesses around the

16
00:01:33,006 --> 00:01:33,417
field.

17
00:01:33,617 --> 00:01:39,317
The local magnetic field sets the Larmor frequency;
here we slow that precession.

18
00:01:39,517 --> 00:01:45,542
A resonant transverse RF field rotates the spin
state, changing the moment’s direction.

19
00:01:45,742 --> 00:01:51,717
Now fill this three-dimensional grid with identical
small samples of hydrogen spins.

20
00:01:51,917 --> 00:01:57,567
Each sample sees the same ideal field and pulse, so
all net moments tip together.

21
00:01:57,767 --> 00:02:04,724
After excitation, longitudinal magnetization
recovers through energy exchange with the molecular

22
00:02:04,724 --> 00:02:05,667
surroundings.

23
00:02:05,867 --> 00:02:12,817
T one is the time to recover about sixty-three
percent of the missing longitudinal magnetization.

24
00:02:13,017 --> 00:02:20,267
Transverse coherence also decays: T two leaves about
thirty-seven percent after one time constant.

25
00:02:20,467 --> 00:02:26,217
Now two materials have different relaxation times
and local frequency offsets.

26
00:02:26,417 --> 00:02:33,026
Molecular motion and fluctuating magnetic
interactions influence relaxation; shielding and

27
00:02:33,026 --> 00:02:35,817
susceptibility affect local resonance.

28
00:02:36,017 --> 00:02:42,926
Electrical permittivity influences RF-field
distribution; it alone does not determine these

29
00:02:42,926 --> 00:02:44,217
relaxation times.

30
00:02:44,417 --> 00:02:51,192
During RF excitation, G z makes resonance frequency
vary with z across this volume.

31
00:02:51,392 --> 00:02:57,392
The RF frequency band excites a slab; these outer
moments remain longitudinal.

32
00:02:57,592 --> 00:03:03,617
During readout, G x makes precession frequency vary
with x throughout the excited slice.

33
00:03:03,817 --> 00:03:10,257
Positions sharing x contribute at the same
frequency, so this measurement alone combines their

34
00:03:10,257 --> 00:03:11,217
contributions.

35
00:03:11,417 --> 00:03:16,842
A brief G y pulse accumulates different phases at
different y positions.

36
00:03:17,042 --> 00:03:23,517
When G y ends, its frequency shift ends, while the
accumulated phase differences remain.

37
00:03:23,717 --> 00:03:28,817
Follow these two samples: they share x but occupy
different y positions.

38
00:03:29,017 --> 00:03:35,542
Their first measurement adds both contributions:
point eight plus point three gives one point one.

39
00:03:35,742 --> 00:03:42,367
A second phase encoding makes their relative phase
half a turn; their contributions now subtract.

40
00:03:42,567 --> 00:03:48,754
Combining those two measurements recovers each
contribution; many y positions require more

41
00:03:48,754 --> 00:03:50,267
independent encodings.

42
00:03:50,467 --> 00:03:57,349
Each k-space sample combines the excited slice; a
complete set reconstructs the spatial voxel

43
00:03:57,349 --> 00:03:57,867
values.

44
00:03:58,067 --> 00:04:03,742
This small white box marks a block of the hand, five
by five by ten millimetres.

45
00:04:03,942 --> 00:04:09,617
Here that hand sample fills the grid; Sync can match
its orientation to the scanner.

46
00:04:09,817 --> 00:04:16,017
Use Sync to match the hand’s orientation, or unlock
it and turn the tissue independently.

47
00:04:16,217 --> 00:04:21,492
This three-dimensional grid has six by six by eight
sampled tissue moments.

48
00:04:21,692 --> 00:04:27,042
Each arrow initially shows the net magnetization of
a small tissue sample.

49
00:04:27,242 --> 00:04:33,467
The M control switches between net magnetization and
an illustrative microscopic moment.

50
00:04:33,667 --> 00:04:37,817
The Phase control colours the moments by their
transverse phase.

51
00:04:38,017 --> 00:04:43,467
The arrows change direction and length as the
sample’s magnetization changes.

52
00:04:43,667 --> 00:04:47,692
Select a moment to follow that sample in the
enlarged views.

53
00:04:47,892 --> 00:04:53,092
This circle carries the colours: each colour is one
direction across the field.

54
00:04:53,292 --> 00:04:57,892
This plot will show each coil's current against
time: the pulse sequence.

55
00:04:58,092 --> 00:05:02,067
This plot will show the signal measured by the
receive loop.

56
00:05:02,267 --> 00:05:05,917
And here the measurements will collect and become an
image.

57
00:05:06,917 --> 00:05:13,067
In this field, protons precess at 42.6 megahertz:
the Larmor frequency.

58
00:05:13,267 --> 00:05:17,817
Their turning is shown slowed here, to one turn
every two seconds.

59
00:05:18,017 --> 00:05:23,992
Now the RF coil's current makes a weak field, B₁,
turning at that same frequency.

60
00:05:24,217 --> 00:05:27,742
These coral arrows show B₁ filling the whole coil.

61
00:05:28,417 --> 00:05:33,667
The second view turns with that field, and time runs
ten thousand times slower.

62
00:05:33,867 --> 00:05:39,517
Near resonance, the rotating drive steadily tips the
tissue’s net magnetization.

63
00:05:39,717 --> 00:05:45,467
The tipping adds up, though this field is 85
thousand times weaker than the magnet's.

64
00:05:45,667 --> 00:05:51,942
Transverse magnetization is now strong, with
matching phases across the excited tissue.

65
00:05:52,958 --> 00:05:58,883
In the scanner, the tipped protons sweep round 42.6
million times a second.

66
00:05:59,158 --> 00:06:03,808
Changing magnetic flux induces a voltage in the
receive loop.

67
00:06:04,008 --> 00:06:13,183
This example receiver digitizes the radio-frequency
voltage at 122.88 million samples per second.

68
00:06:13,383 --> 00:06:20,608
A digital oscillator supplies sine and cosine
references. Mixing shifts the signal toward
baseband.

69
00:06:20,808 --> 00:06:27,683
Filtering and decimation leave 32 thousand I and Q
pairs per second in this example.

70
00:06:27,883 --> 00:06:34,958
I and Q together describe the received signal,
weighted by the coil sensitivity across the sample.

71
00:06:35,158 --> 00:06:39,458
Its colour is its phase, the same colours as the
protons.

72
00:06:39,658 --> 00:06:42,658
Now time runs a hundred times faster.

73
00:06:42,858 --> 00:06:47,333
Each pair joins this line in the signal plot, drawn
against time.

74
00:06:47,533 --> 00:06:53,883
The signal shrinks: across the hand the field
differs by millionths, so protons drift apart.

75
00:06:54,083 --> 00:07:01,433
Within each tissue sample, transverse coherence
decays while longitudinal magnetization recovers.

76
00:07:01,633 --> 00:07:05,483
Muscle's tipped part fades within tens of
milliseconds.

77
00:07:05,683 --> 00:07:11,958
Fat's fades more slowly, and fat's protons turn
slightly slower, so their colour drifts.

78
00:07:13,358 --> 00:07:17,808
After a pause, the protons are back along the field
and tipped again.

79
00:07:18,008 --> 00:07:21,358
These are the z gradient coils, in violet.

80
00:07:21,558 --> 00:07:25,608
Their current appears in the pulse sequence, on the
Gz row.

81
00:07:25,808 --> 00:07:30,858
It makes the field grow along the bore: the lines
brighten where it is stronger.

82
00:07:31,058 --> 00:07:35,383
In the block, this violet arrow shows the field
rising along z.

83
00:07:35,583 --> 00:07:40,783
Each plane now precesses at its own frequency, so
the colours twist along z.

84
00:07:40,983 --> 00:07:47,533
When the gradient stops, its frequency shift ends.
The accumulated phase differences remain.

85
00:07:47,733 --> 00:07:53,283
With phases fanned out, the protons add up to almost
nothing: the signal vanishes.

86
00:07:53,483 --> 00:07:56,483
Reversing the current unwinds the twist.

87
00:07:59,083 --> 00:08:03,258
The colours line up again, and the signal returns:
an echo.

88
00:08:04,692 --> 00:08:08,592
After another pause, the z coils carry current
again.

89
00:08:08,792 --> 00:08:12,917
Each plane's frequency now rises steadily along the
bore.

90
00:08:13,117 --> 00:08:18,792
With the z gradient active, this pulse’s centre
frequency targets the middle plane.

91
00:08:19,992 --> 00:08:23,092
Its field reaches the whole volume at once.

92
00:08:24,197 --> 00:08:31,147
This pulse chiefly excites a 1500-hertz band, with a
finite transition at the slice edges.

93
00:08:31,347 --> 00:08:36,997
Farther off resonance, the pulse produces much less
net tipping across the tissue.

94
00:08:41,693 --> 00:08:45,468
The tipped protons form a slab 5 millimetres thick.

95
00:08:45,668 --> 00:08:51,818
Its thickness is the pulse's bandwidth divided by
how fast frequency changes along z.

96
00:08:52,018 --> 00:08:55,968
Through the slab's thickness, their colours have
drifted apart.

97
00:08:56,168 --> 00:09:02,943
A reversed z-gradient lobe supplies the rephasing
area needed after this slice-selective pulse.

98
00:09:13,583 --> 00:09:16,933
These are the x gradient coils, in cyan.

99
00:09:21,383 --> 00:09:25,633
A short reversed pulse first winds the slab's
colours along x.

100
00:09:33,283 --> 00:09:37,133
Then the current flips, and the receiver starts
sampling.

101
00:09:37,333 --> 00:09:41,658
The field now rises along x, as the cyan arrow
shows.

102
00:09:41,858 --> 00:09:46,733
So protons at each x precess at their own rate,
unwinding the twist.

103
00:09:46,933 --> 00:09:54,258
At the echo, the readout gradient refocuses its
accumulated x phase, and the combined signal peaks.

104
00:09:54,458 --> 00:09:58,683
Every sample is the sum over the whole slab at that
instant.

105
00:09:58,883 --> 00:10:04,808
Protons sharing an x but not a y turn alike, so this
readout cannot separate them.

106
00:10:05,008 --> 00:10:09,733
These samples fill one row of a table of
measurements, called k-space.

107
00:10:09,933 --> 00:10:14,408
Along the row, kx counts the turns of x twist at
each sample.

108
00:10:15,817 --> 00:10:19,392
After a pause, the same slab is excited again.

109
00:10:19,592 --> 00:10:24,042
This time the y gradient coils, in orange, carry a
pulse first.

110
00:10:24,242 --> 00:10:26,742
The field now rises along y.

111
00:10:26,942 --> 00:10:31,567
Protons higher up precess faster, and the colours
twist along y.

112
00:10:31,767 --> 00:10:34,467
When the current stops, the twist stays.

113
00:10:34,667 --> 00:10:40,667
The gradient pulse area sets ky, a spatial frequency
measured in cycles per metre.

114
00:10:40,867 --> 00:10:43,492
The readout then runs as before.

115
00:10:55,767 --> 00:11:00,942
Each sample now adds up the slab's protons with the
y twist included.

116
00:11:09,967 --> 00:11:13,092
These samples fill a different row of k-space.

117
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:19,200
The scanner repeats this, each time with a different
y pulse.

118
00:11:19,400 --> 00:11:22,900
Here each repetition is shown only at its echo.

119
00:11:23,100 --> 00:11:26,950
More turns fill rows farther from the middle of
k-space.

120
00:11:27,150 --> 00:11:31,600
The image forms where the slab is, sharpening as
rows arrive.

121
00:11:31,800 --> 00:11:36,675
For each point, the samples are added again with
that point's twists undone.

122
00:11:36,875 --> 00:11:43,882
Combining the encodings estimates each voxel. Finite
sampling sets the resolution and point-spread

123
00:11:43,882 --> 00:11:44,525
function.

124
00:11:44,725 --> 00:11:50,100
Rows with few turns carry the most signal; outer
rows add the fine detail.

125
00:11:51,125 --> 00:11:55,900
Shifting the RF frequency moves the matching plane
along the bore.

126
00:11:56,100 --> 00:11:59,700
Each slab is excited and measured the same way.

127
00:11:59,900 --> 00:12:05,450
The complete stack covers the hand, with each slice
added only after it is measured.

128
00:12:05,650 --> 00:12:10,275
Its brightness comes from each tissue's protons and
how fast they relax.

129
00:12:10,475 --> 00:12:17,125
Cortical bone has very short-lived signal. Tendons
also appear dark with the timing used here.

130
00:12:17,325 --> 00:12:23,575
These measurements and images were calculated from
the simulated anatomy and acquisition.

131
00:12:25,583 --> 00:12:32,613
Explore Resonance MRI yourself in virtual or mixed
reality, or on Windows. The project is free and

132
00:12:32,613 --> 00:12:39,787
open source. Scan this code for downloads, source
code and Meta Quest release updates. Find the same

133
00:12:39,787 --> 00:12:41,508
link in the description.
